# 02 — Literal Types & Union Types

## What is this?
A literal type represents an exact specific value (e.g. `"admin"`, `200`, `true`). A union type (`|`) represents a value that can be one of several types. Combining literal types with union types allows you to model finite sets of acceptable values with compile-time verification.

## Why does TypeScript have it?
- Replaces magic strings and arbitrary numbers with strict, compiler-enforced enums and state representations.

## Basic Syntax
```typescript
// String literal union
type Status = "pending" | "active" | "archived";

// Number literal union
type HttpSuccessCode = 200 | 201 | 204;

// Type narrowing
function handleStatus(status: Status) {
    if (status === "active") {
        // TypeScript knows status is strictly "active" here
    }
}
```

## How it Behaves
1. Exact Value Matching: A variable typed as `"asc" | "desc"` will reject `"ascending"` at compile time.
2. Union Narrowing: Inside conditional blocks (`if`, `switch`), TypeScript narrows the broad union down to the specific branch value.

## Common Mistakes
1. Forgetting to export the type alias: When using types across multiple files, remember `export type ...`.
2. Assuming literal types are preserved in mutable objects without `as const`:
   ```typescript
   const req = { method: "GET" }; // inferred as { method: string }
   // Fix: const req = { method: "GET" as const };
   ```

## Small Practical Example
```typescript
type Direction = "north" | "south" | "east" | "west";

function move(dir: Direction, steps: number): string {
    return `Moving ${steps} steps towards ${dir}`;
}
```

## What to Practice
Implement in `exercise.ts`:
1. Define type alias `LogLevel = "debug" | "info" | "warn" | "error"`.
2. Define type alias `HttpStatus = 200 | 400 | 401 | 404 | 500`.
3. `isClientError(status: HttpStatus): boolean`: Returns `true` if `status` is `400`, `401`, or `404`, otherwise `false`.
4. `formatLog(level: LogLevel, message: string): string`: Returns `"[<LEVEL>]: <message>"`, where `<LEVEL>` is uppercase (e.g. `"[INFO]: Server started"`).

## Progressive Hints
- Hint 1: Export type `LogLevel` and `HttpStatus`.
- Hint 2: In `isClientError`, check `status >= 400 && status < 500`.

## Reference Solution

<details>
<summary>Click to reveal reference solution</summary>

```typescript
export type LogLevel = "debug" | "info" | "warn" | "error";
export type HttpStatus = 200 | 400 | 401 | 404 | 500;

export function isClientError(status: HttpStatus): boolean {
    return status === 400 || status === 401 || status === 404;
}

export function formatLog(level: LogLevel, message: string): string {
    return `[${level.toUpperCase()}]: ${message}`;
}
```
</details>

