# 12 — Template Literal Types

## What is this?
Template literal types build on string literal types and have the ability to expand into many strings via unions: `` `${Prefix}_${Suffix}` ``. TypeScript provides built-in string manipulation utility types: `Uppercase<S>`, `Lowercase<S>`, `Capitalize<S>`, and `Uncapitalize<S>`.

## Why does TypeScript have it?
- Allows defining strictly typed event names (e.g. `"onUserCreated"`, `"onUserDeleted"`), CSS class mappings, and type-safe routing patterns.

## Basic Syntax
```typescript
type Protocol = "http" | "https";
type Domain = "example.com" | "internal.dev";

// Cross-product expansion: "http://example.com" | "http://internal.dev" | ...
type WebUrl = `${Protocol}://${Domain}`;

type EventType = "click" | "hover";
type EventHandlerName = `on${Capitalize<EventType>}`; // "onClick" | "onHover"
```

## How it Behaves
1. Cross-Product Combinations: If multiple unions are interpolated in a single template literal, TypeScript generates all permutations automatically.
2. Pattern Matching with `infer`: You can parse strings at compile time using template literals and `infer` (e.g. extracting path parameters from `"/users/:id"`).

## Common Mistakes
1. Generating massive unions: Combining 4 large unions can create millions of combinations, causing compiler slow-downs.

## Small Practical Example
```typescript
type HttpMethod = "get" | "post";
type ApiRoute = `/${string}`;
type Endpoint = `${Uppercase<HttpMethod>} ${ApiRoute}`;
```

