# 14 — Abstract Classes & Polymorphism

## What is this?
An `abstract class` is a base class that cannot be instantiated directly with `new`. It serves as a blueprint that can contain both implemented methods (with shared logic) and `abstract` method signatures that concrete derived classes must implement.

## Why does TypeScript have it?
- Implements the Template Method pattern: common lifecycle logic is defined in the abstract base class, while specific steps are delegated to subclasses.

## Basic Syntax
```typescript
abstract class NotificationService {
    abstract send(recipient: string, message: string): boolean;

    // Shared template method
    public notifyWithTimestamp(recipient: string, message: string): boolean {
        const stamped = `[${new Date().toISOString()}] ${message}`;
        return this.send(recipient, stamped);
    }
}

class EmailService extends NotificationService {
    send(recipient: string, message: string): boolean {
        console.log(`Email to ${recipient}: ${message}`);
        return true;
    }
}
```

## How it Behaves
1. Instantiation Block: Calling `new AbstractClass()` causes a compile-time error.
2. Polymorphism: Functions can accept the abstract base class type, allowing any derived subclass to be passed interchangeably.

## Common Mistakes
1. Forgetting to implement an abstract method in a derived class: Triggers compile error `Non-abstract class 'X' does not implement inherited abstract member 'Y'`.

## Small Practical Example
```typescript
abstract class Command {
    abstract execute(): void;
}
```

