# 18 — Utility Types: Exclude, Extract, ReturnType

## What is this?
TypeScript provides type-level operators that manipulate unions and function types: `Exclude<T, U>`, `Extract<T, U>`, `NonNullable<T>`, `ReturnType<T>`, `Parameters<T>`, and `Awaited<T>`.

## Why does TypeScript have it?
- Allows composing and transforming types dynamically from existing function signatures or union definitions without code duplication.

## Basic Syntax
```typescript
type Status = "pending" | "approved" | "rejected" | "canceled";

// Remove elements from union
type ActiveStatus = Exclude<Status, "canceled" | "rejected">; // "pending" | "approved"

// Extract elements from union
type ResolvedStatus = Extract<Status, "approved" | "rejected">; // "approved" | "rejected"

// Inspect function types
function createUser(name: string, age: number) {
    return { id: 1, name, age };
}

type User = ReturnType<typeof createUser>; // { id: number; name: string; age: number }
type UserArgs = Parameters<typeof createUser>; // [name: string, age: number]
```

## How it Behaves
1. `Exclude<T, U>`: Distributes over `T` and excludes any members that are assignable to `U`.
2. `NonNullable<T>`: Excludes `null` and `undefined` from `T`.
3. `ReturnType<T>`: Extracts the return type of a function type `T`.

## Common Mistakes
1. Passing a function value directly to `ReturnType` instead of its type: `ReturnType<myFunc>` is an error; use `ReturnType<typeof myFunc>`.

## Small Practical Example
```typescript
type Primitive = string | number | boolean | null | undefined;
type DefinitePrimitive = NonNullable<Primitive>; // string | number | boolean
```

